{
  "instrument": {
    "state": "Act on overlapping customer histories: dated order sequences and, for a limited consenting sample, reconstructed sequences of receipt or collection, product use, stock remaining, attempted refilling and subsequent purchase. Order dates provide calendar anchors; recalled events may have uncertain dates, omitted episodes or unusual quantities. Sample both repeat purchasers and nonpurchasers across fulfillment options, starter versus existing containers, discounts and acquisition periods. Shared service conditions might produce recurring temporal patterns, but neither comparable household consumption nor a common response to those conditions is established.",
    "operation": "During week one, construct a calendar-dated order reference and separate weekly event sequences for participating households. Use one voluntary invitation offering an interview and optional agreed follow-up; do not repeatedly contact nonrespondents. Ask about actual use, remaining stock, alternative purchases and service encounters without presuming dissatisfaction. Preserve unknown intervals and distinguish reported events from recorded orders. Compare overlapping histories, shifting only uncertain recalled segments within their reported date ranges until distinctive ordered patterns correspond across several independently anchored histories. Keep known order dates fixed, preserve event order, and compare alternative alignments rather than selecting the most convenient one. For example, test whether uncertain recollections of a failed collection fall into a consistently dated sequence of low stock, collection difficulty and delayed purchase. Also compare time since first order and since reported depletion, without replacing calendar dates. Where a mismatch suggests a missing episode, seek clarification through an already agreed follow-up; do not insert an event merely to improve the match. Stop alignment when alternatives remain equally plausible, overlap is insufficient or the first week ends. If a recurring, corroborated sequence identifies an actionable fulfillment or communication obstacle, choose one corresponding process change for the remaining month: for example, clearer refill instructions for evidenced confusion, or a feasible collection-hours adjustment for evidenced access difficulty. Prespecify its intended observable effect and, where feasible, randomly allocate eligible customers or collection periods to the changed process and the current process. If no explanation is sufficiently discriminated, stop short of choosing a purported remedy and use the purchasing deadline to make a cautious, explicitly uncertain commitment from confirmed orders and reported stock needs.",
    "signal": "Look for one permissible alignment that repeatedly matches distinctive sequences across independently anchored households and makes previously inconsistent timing intelligible. Record whether competing alignments also fit. A candidate signal could be low stock followed by an unsuccessful collection attempt and a later order, recurring around the same service conditions; another could be continued stock availability without a refill attempt. These are proposed observations, not findings. Apparent missing or anomalous episodes become questions for corroboration, not established facts. During the trial, observe the specific targeted step—such as successful collection or correct completion of refilling—alongside remaining stock, attempted purchases and dated orders. Record participation and missing follow-up separately from outcomes.",
    "inference": "A distinctive, independently corroborated alignment can narrow the calendar position of uncertain customer events, check reconstructed histories and connect overlapping observation periods. It can thereby distinguish some households that have not yet needed a refill from households whose need preceded a documented obstacle. Repeated correspondence supports prioritizing a mechanism for a bounded trial; it does not establish that the mechanism explains the overall decline. A credible comparison during the month can support a provisional decision about the changed process within the tested group and conditions. At five weeks, base the purchasing commitment on confirmed demand, observed stock and usage information, trial results and explicit uncertainty. Use any consented later observation to assess second orders once cohorts have actually completed eight weeks.",
    "limit": "Household size, usage, initial quantity, alternative purchasing and local service events can disrupt shared patterns. Short, repetitive sequences, recall and measurement errors, incorrect anchors, insufficient overlap and searching many shifts can create false matches. Do not claim precise dates when only a range is supported, or extend a chronology beyond supported overlaps. Promotions and new collection points began in the same quarter, so matching timing alone cannot separate their effects. Voluntary participants and support-message senders may differ from other customers; neither complaint frequency, silence nor nonpurchase directly measures satisfaction. A month's trial cannot establish eight-week repeat purchase for newly exposed customers, and small or nonrandom comparisons may remain inconclusive. Stop or revise the trial if it creates observed fulfillment problems or cannot be delivered as specified; document departures before interpreting outcomes. Major packaging redesign is unavailable, and evidence of leaks may warrant further investigation without implying that the permitted process change will resolve them."
  }
}
